### What steps will reproduce the bug?

```ts
const fill = (n, unit) => Buffer.alloc(n * unit.length, unit).toString();
for (const n of [2000, 4000, 8000]) {
  const input = "www.a.bc/" + fill(n, "*www.a.bc/x*") + "(";
  const start = performance.now();
  Bun.markdown.ansi(input, { colors: false });
  console.log(input.length, "bytes:", Math.round(performance.now() - start), "ms");
}
// 24010 bytes: 237 ms
// 48010 bytes: 778 ms
// 96010 bytes: 2707 ms
```

`Bun.markdown.html(input, { autolinks: true })`, `render()` and `react()` take the same time. `Bun.markdown.ansi()` and `bun file.md` have autolinks on by default. Without autolinks the 96 KB input takes 1.6 ms.

### What is the expected behavior?

Linear time. The other pathological inputs of `test/js/bun/md/md-edge-cases.test.ts` render 1 MB in well under a second.

### What do you see instead?

The time grows with the square of the input length: 2.7 s for 96 KB, 6.5 s for 192 KB.

Cause, in `src/md/autolinks.rs`:

- Each `www.` that follows a paired `*` is a link candidate. `scan_url_component` scans its path to the end of the token, because `*`, `.` and `/` are URL bytes of a path.
- The candidate fails at the `(` in `check_right_boundary`, so the inline walk continues at the next byte. The next `www.` lies in the bytes that were just scanned, and it starts the same scan again.
- `post_process_autolink_end` also counts `(` and `)` over the whole candidate for each candidate.

The same happens with an emphasis delimiter that is not paired in the place of the `(`, for example `"www.a.bc/" + fill(n, "*www.a.bc/x*") + "~ "`.

A possible fix: `scan_url_component` accepts a byte from its value and its two neighbors only, so a scan that starts in the range of the last scan of the same component (path, query, fragment) ends where that scan ended. A memo of that range, and a count of the parentheses that does not walk the whole candidate, make the work linear.

Related: #42327 changes which candidates are valid next to emphasis. It does not change this scan, and its description lists one more input that reaches it.

