```

The value never changes after the stream opens. `util.inspect(stream)` shows the same number.

### Additional information

**Cause.** `get_stream_state` reads `Stream.window_size` ([h2_frame_parser.rs:4977](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/blob/29d9638da3dd5b498a5b608d3fa02549b0bdddf1/src/runtime/api/bun/h2_frame_parser.rs#L4977)). `Stream::init` sets that field once, from an estimate in `handle_received_stream_id`. The window is in the engine: `Connection.streams[id].recv_window` (`size` and `consumed`, `src/runtime/api/bun/h2/flow_control.rs`). Node reads `nghttp2_session_get_stream_local_window_size()` on each access, which is `max(0, size - consumed)`.

**Why the fix is not one line.** `state` is read most often inside a frame callback (`'stream'`, `'data'`, `'localSettings'`). `rewrite_read` keeps the engine `RefCell` mutably borrowed while those callbacks run, so the getter cannot read the engine there. The fix depends on what happens to that borrow:

- #39873 runs the frame callbacks after the borrow is released. The getter can then read the engine with `try_borrow()`. Only a read from inside the `_write` of a JS transport, during a socket read, still finds the engine borrowed.
- If callbacks stay under the borrow, the getter needs one of these:
  - a copy of the window on the parser's `Stream`, written by a new `Sink` callback. That is one more stream lookup on each DATA frame, for every session.
  - a read-only view of the engine that each `Sink` callback receives. That is a new rule for the `Sink` trait and one `unsafe` deref in the file that #40240 makes free of `unsafe`.

The branch [`robobun/bae51a65/h2-stream-local-window-size`](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/compare/robobun/2ad1bff9/h2-recv-window-settings-shrink...robobun/bae51a65/h2-stream-local-window-size) has a working version of the last option on top of #41329, with 11 tests. It prints `64535 1024`. It is not a pull request, because it goes against the direction of #39873 and #40240. `Connection::stream_recv_credit()` and 10 of the 11 tests do not depend on how the getter reaches the engine.

**Two more things that a fix needs.**

- #41329 (or an equal change). Without it the engine does not move the stream windows when the peer ACKs a new `initialWindowSize`, so the second number stays wrong.
- A stream WINDOW_UPDATE check when the engine applies that ACK. Without it the second number is 24 inside `'localSettings'` and 1024 after the read. The commit is offered on #41329.

**Values that match node with the branch above** (a raw peer, each frame in its own socket read):

| case | values |
| --- | --- |
| DATA of 16384, 16384 and 7232 bytes, read inside `'data'` | 49151, 32767, 58303 |
| 1000 bytes with 10 bytes of padding, then 100 bytes that are all padding | 64524, then 64424 |
| a 200000-byte window, 81920 bytes that nobody reads, an ACKed window of 1024, then of 100000, then `resume()` | 118080, 0, 18080, 100000 |
| the server starts with 1000. Stream 1 opens before the ACK, stream 3 after it | stream 1: 65535, then 1000. Stream 3: 1000 |
| a client request with no frame from the server, then an ACKed window of 2048 | 65535, then 2048 |
| a client stream inside `'end'`, after a 9-byte response | 65526 |

**Values that still differ from node with any of these fixes.** The getter is then the same read as in node. The engine differs in when it sends a stream WINDOW_UPDATE, and in when it marks a stream closed.

- Three DATA frames in one socket read. Node checks after each chunk, and bun at the end of the read. Inside the third `'data'` event node reports 58303 and bun 25535.
- A DATA frame that arrives in two socket reads. Bun counts the whole frame when its header arrives.
- A paused stream that buffers less than the `highWaterMark` of its Readable. Bun still sends a WINDOW_UPDATE, and node does not.
- A stream that is closed while its data is unread. Node reports state 1 and a window of 0. Bun reports state 7 and the window that was left. #43801 changes what `state` reports for a closed stream.

Nobody has reported a program that reads this value. This came out of the review of #41329.

