### Additional information

The cause is the order of two steps in `H2FrameParser::rewrite_read` (`src/runtime/api/bun/h2_frame_parser.rs`).

- `rewrite_read` keeps the engine `RefCell` borrowed while frame callbacks run JS. A `setLocalWindowSize()` call made inside a callback cannot reach the engine. It writes the WINDOW_UPDATE and adds the growth to `pending_recv_window_growth`.
- `rewrite_read` applies `pending_recv_window_growth` only at its start.
- The response write overflows the 16384 byte cork, so the WINDOW_UPDATE reaches the JS transport inside the same callback. The peer answers synchronously. That nested `read()` finds the engine borrowed and appends its bytes to `rewrite_tail`.
- After the outer `receive()` returns, the drain loop at the end of `rewrite_read` feeds `rewrite_tail` to the engine. It does not apply the pending growth first. The engine checks the DATA against the old window of 65535 bytes and ends the session.

The invariant that is missing: every change that the binding makes is in the engine before each `Connection::receive()` call, the drain loop included.

Open pull requests that touch this:

- #41329 moves the sync block into `sync_engine()` and calls it before each drain. That also applies the pending growth. It does not mention or test this case.
- #41344 makes the engine pull queued window changes, and removes `pending_recv_window_growth`.
- #39873 rewrites `rewrite_read` and removes the drain loop that #41329 changes. The new loop needs the same invariant.

This was found in review of #40180. That PR does not change this path. Node v26.3.0 does not support this transport: it aborts when a transport delivers bytes from inside its own write.

