### What steps can reproduce the bug?

In Bun, the `tls.TLSSocket.end(cb)` callback fires before the plaintext backlog has been flushed to a backpressured wrapped stream. The same code running in Node only fires the callback once the backlog has been flushed to the stream.

In the code below, a server-side `TLSSocket` wraps a duplex that queues writes with callbacks while backpressured. Right after the TLS handshake the duplex is backpressured, then the server writes one byte which gets stuck in the backlog, and calls `end(cb)`.

`node`: `cb` fires only after the backlog drains; client gets the byte (`PASS`)
`bun`:  `cb` fires immediately; client gets nothing (`FAIL`)

The code below shows the server direction, but note that this occurs in the client direction as well. `write(cb)` on the wrapped stream is also affected the same way (the callback fires before the wrapped stream accepts the chunk).

